          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"zuccoli-lamore-di-loredana-0284ce0a69768fff\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"The complete core utterance is the negated future declaration “Non vi amerò mai” (“I shall never love you”). It primarily presents as true Loredana’s enduring absence of love for Berto. It also has a weak performative shade as a categorical rejection of his courtship, but it does not clearly promise or undertake an obligation. Nothing indicates that the words themselves are a reflex-like affective discharge rather than a forcefully delivered avowal and refusal.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The future construction and categorical “mai” can sound somewhat commissive or boundary-setting, but the dominant force is truth-conditional prediction/avowal rather than an undertaking.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Loredana directly addresses Berto in dialogue; the classified event is the whole negated utterance “Non vi amerò mai,” not the affirmative substring “vi amerò” in isolation.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Loredana interrupts Berto’s declaration of love and his hope of replacing Filippo with an unequivocal rejection. Her repeated statements that she loves only Filippo and has not come because she loves Berto strongly support a literal denial of present and future love. Her agitation and feverish physical state intensify the scene but do not independently give the utterance expressive/reflexive force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“--No,--rispose.--Non vi amerò mai.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit negation and “mai” make this a categorical assertion that Loredana will never love Berto, with a secondary courtship-rejecting function.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Loredana says of Filippo: “Lo amo ancora, lo amo sempre, non amo che lui.”\",\"supports\":\"Her avowed exclusive and continuing love for Filippo corroborates the literal truth-conditional reading of her denial toward Berto.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately afterward she explains: “Sono qui, non perchè vi ami.”\",\"supports\":\"She explicitly denies that love for Berto motivates her presence, reinforcing the utterance as an avowal of non-love rather than an expressive ejaculation.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Berto has just said that he will make her forget Filippo and declares, “Vi amo teneramente.”\",\"supports\":\"The target utterance directly rejects Berto’s romantic proposal and expectations, accounting for its limited performative or boundary-setting shade.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration describes Loredana as feverish and violently agitated, but she deliberately cuts Berto off and then continues with an explanation of her motives.\",\"supports\":\"The emotional intensity is contextual delivery evidence only; the organized responsive denial supplies no positive evidence of reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the categorical avowal/prediction of non-love, while a small P score captures its secondary function as a firm relational refusal. Contextual agitation can be described without assigning E, and no important aspect of the core force falls outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The metadata’s extracted affirmative span occurs inside a syntactically decisive negation; classification therefore concerns the full sentence “Non vi amerò mai.”\"}"
        }
      ],
